WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.650
It's been nearly three decades since the release
of "Pulp Fiction," and it's still one of the

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:09.345
most beloved films of all time — even though
one of the lead actors' parents didn't appreciate it. 

00:00:09.345 --> 00:00:13.459
Here are some fascinating facts about
Quentin Tarantino's violent classic.

00:00:13.459 --> 00:00:17.400
John Travolta joined the cast of "Pulp Fiction"
during a low point in his career. He started

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.060
out strong in the '70s, parlaying his success
in the TV smash hit "Welcome Back, Kotter"

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:26.369
into even bigger roles in films like "Grease,"
"Saturday Night Fever," and "Blow Out" — the

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:31.060
last of which Quentin Tarantino considers
one of his top three movies ever made. Regardless,

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:34.470
he hadn't made a movie like that in quite
some time when he got a call from Tarantino

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:39.000
in the early '90s. After the success of his
debut feature, "Reservoir Dogs," the filmmaker

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.958
had the clout to live out his dream and make
his idol a star again.

00:00:41.958 --> 00:00:47.478
That's a very kind gesture, but the way Travolta
tells the story makes it sound almost creepy.

00:00:47.478 --> 00:00:51.121
During an appearance at the Supanova Comic
Con &amp; Gaming event in Adelaide, the actor

00:00:51.121 --> 00:00:55.410
shared his tale of being invited to Tarantino's
house and discovering his collection of Travolta-themed

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.850
board games. Travolta said —

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:01.690
"He just had this fantasy of being with his
favorite actor and playing the board games

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:03.300
from that actor's movies."

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:07.509
Travolta, being a bit eccentric himself, found
this odd behavior thoughtful and charming

00:01:07.509 --> 00:01:09.158
rather than unnerving.

00:01:09.158 --> 00:01:13.270
"Pulp Fiction" is one of the most quotable
movies ever made, but few lines have stuck

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:16.950
in viewers' heads quite like the monologue
that Samuel L. Jackson's character Jules delivers

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:21.580
to his victims — eventually inspiring him
to leave crime behind. He claims to be quoting

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:25.937
scripture, specifically a passage he says
is called Ezekiel 25:17.

00:01:25.937 --> 00:01:33.023
"And you will know my name is the Lord when
I lay my vengeance upon thee."

00:01:36.994 --> 00:01:40.990
If you know how biblical citation works, you'll
notice Jules speaks for a lot longer than

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:45.360
one verse — and in fact, only the ending
of the speech comes from the source Jules

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.820
claims for it. The rest is a mishmash of Old
and New Testament, with sources ranging from

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:54.200
Genesis to the 23rd Psalm. It seems like a
classic example of Quentin Tarantino's irreverent

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:59.112
mix-and-match approach to writing — and
it is, but he didn't actually write the monologue at all.

00:01:59.112 --> 00:02:03.640
Jules' speech actually comes from "Bodigaado
Kiba" — "The Bodyguard" in English — a

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:08.270
low-budget comic book adaptation full of crime
and violence. Starring the legendary martial

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:12.878
artist Sonny Chiba, a new narrated text prologue
was added when the film was released in English.

00:02:12.878 --> 00:02:17.510
Right down to the bogus citation, it's almost
word-for-word identical to Jules' big speech

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:21.380
— although Tarantino naturally had to take
out the reference to Chiba's character.

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:28.599
"And they shall know that I am Chiba the Bodyguard
when I shall lay my vengeance upon them."

00:02:28.599 --> 00:02:31.940
Tarantino would eventually get the chance
to work with Chiba during production on "Kill

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:35.732
Bill," in which the Japanese actor plays the
master swordsmith Hattori Hanzo.

00:02:35.732 --> 00:02:39.680
Actor Eric Stoltz appears in "Pulp Fiction"
as Lance, the local drug dealer who supplies

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.620
Vince with the drugs that almost kill Mia
Wallace. In one of the film's most memorable

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:47.752
moments, he has to show the frantic hitman
how to revive her with a syringe of adrenaline

00:02:47.752 --> 00:02:53.145
— but before all that, Lance is lounging
on the sofa watching TV with a bowl of cereal in hand.

00:02:53.145 --> 00:02:56.865
The specific brand is the kind of detail most
directors wouldn't think twice about, but

00:02:56.865 --> 00:03:01.870
it was serious business to Tarantino. He chose
Fruit Brute, the long-discontinued monster-themed

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:05.940
cereal that used to adorn grocery-store shelves
during the Halloween season along with its

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:09.650
more famous counterparts Count Chocula and
Frankenberry. The brand went out of regular

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:14.270
circulation eight Halloweens after its debut
and was soon forgotten by just about everyone

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:19.800
— except for Tarantino, apparently. General
Mills did eventually bring Fruit Brute back

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.459
to store shelves in 2013 to haunt your breakfast
table, but that was nearly 20 years after

00:03:24.459 --> 00:03:26.299
"Pulp Fiction's" release.

00:03:26.299 --> 00:03:29.880
This wasn't even the first time the director
snuck the discontinued brand into one of his

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:34.220
movies, as you can see a box of it in Mr.
Orange's apartment in "Reservoir Dogs."

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:40.020
For over 25 years, "Pulp Fiction" fans have
wanted to know just one thing: what exactly

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:44.730
is inside that glowing briefcase? All we know
is that Jules and Vince are willing to kill

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:49.349
for it — and even though they open it up
multiple times, we never actually see inside

00:03:49.349 --> 00:03:52.311
it or get any hint other than the unearthly
glow.

00:03:52.311 --> 00:03:54.076
"Is that what I think it is?"

00:03:54.076 --> 00:03:55.787
"Mm-hm."

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:00.000
"It's beautiful."

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.060
That mystery is an essential part of "Pulp
Fiction's" lasting appeal, but Tarantino started

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:08.090
out with a much more concrete answer. His
early drafts with Roger Avary revealed that

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:11.760
the briefcase contained the same diamonds
that the hapless heisters from "Reservoir

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:16.150
Dogs" had been fighting to get their hands
on. Apparently, that was considered "too predictable,"

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:18.600
so the writers decided to leave it up to the
audience.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:23.590
It's an idea that proves Tarantino steals
from the best. The 1955 film noir classic

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:28.160
"Kiss Me Deadly" also revolves around a mysterious
case, and we never see anything but a glow

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:32.450
coming from inside that one either. We know
that particular film was on Tarantino's mind

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:35.193
as he wrote "Pulp Fiction," as he told Sight
and Sound —

00:04:35.193 --> 00:04:38.870
"I wanted [Bruce Willis' character Butch]
to be basically like Ralph Meeker as Mike

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:40.930
Hammer in [Robert] Aldrich's 'Kiss Me Deadly.'"

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:45.120
"Pulp Fiction" restored John Travolta's cool
when he was cast as the character Vincent

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:50.150
Vega. The man has cool clothes, cool dialogue
— and, of course, a cool car. Tarantino

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:53.840
certainly thinks so, as that's his own red
Chevy that Vince is driving.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:58.340
According to Hot Cars, the director bought
the '64 Chevy Malibu Super Sport five years

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:02.039
earlier with the money he made selling the
script for "True Romance." It was part of

00:05:02.039 --> 00:05:05.860
the Chevelle series — they were Chevrolet's
first muscle cars, and they became a favorite

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:07.279
for classic car fans.

00:05:07.279 --> 00:05:11.509
That made it a tempting target for carjackers,
and the Malibu went missing from Tarantino's

00:05:11.509 --> 00:05:15.870
LA home the year "Pulp Fiction" premiered.
The director probably assumed he'd never get

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:22.120
it back, but he did — over 15 years later.
In 2013, TMZ reported that the Malibu was

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:27.200
found. Its owner at the time wasn't charged,
which only seems fair — after all, it probably

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:30.590
changed hands many times since the original
thief got ahold of it.

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:35.000
There's nothing Quentin Tarantino loves more
than dropping references to other movies into

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:39.819
his own, and the twist contest in Jackrabbit
Slim's is full of them. On the Blu-ray release's

00:05:39.819 --> 00:05:43.940
"Chit Chat" documentary extra, John Travolta
recalled that Tarantino showed him Jean-Luc

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:48.479
Godard's "Bande á Part" to pinpoint the vibe
he was looking for. "Pulp Fiction" references

00:05:48.479 --> 00:05:52.250
one of its most famous scenes, in which the
main trio dance to the jukebox in a coffee

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:56.710
shop — but instead of music, there's narration
describing their inner thoughts. "Bande á

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:01.270
Part" obviously made a big impression on Tarantino
— after all, he named his own production

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:02.600
company "A Band Apart."

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:08.265
"Now I wanna dance, I wanna win, I want that
trophy. So dance good."

00:06:08.265 --> 00:06:12.370
Fans of classic movies may also recognize
some of Uma Thurman and Travolta's moves from

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:16.820
an even more important classic of European
cinema. Federico Fellini's "8 ½" combines

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:20.990
fantasy and autobiography for a story of a
filmmaker battling creative frustration and

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:25.940
existential despair. He turns that dour setup
into a whole lot of fun, including a dance

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:29.486
sequence that looks an awful lot like the
one in Jackrabbit Slim's.

00:06:29.486 --> 00:06:33.540
Most people probably don't notice these references
at first glance, but there's another move

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:38.199
that superhero fans might notice. At one point,
Travolta waves two fingers in front of his

00:06:38.199 --> 00:06:42.819
eyes — which is the signature move in "the
Batusi," a dance from the classic '60s "Batman"

00:06:42.819 --> 00:06:44.544
series starring Adam West.

00:06:44.544 --> 00:06:49.680
Vincent Vega has a bad habit of going to the
bathroom at the worst times. When Butch runs

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:52.889
home to pick up his watch, he finds Vince
waiting for him and shoots him as he leaves

00:06:52.889 --> 00:06:57.419
the toilet. Later in the film — but earlier
chronologically — Vince takes another trip

00:06:57.419 --> 00:07:01.590
to the john and misses Jules' confrontation
with the stick-up artists in the diner. Both

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:05.949
times, he's carrying a copy of "Modesty Blaise,"
one of a series of spy thrillers starring

00:07:05.949 --> 00:07:10.740
writer Peter O'Donnell's comic strip heroine.
Modesty was a former war orphan and international

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:13.605
criminal who does freelance work for the British
secret service.

00:07:13.605 --> 00:07:18.357
Quentin Tarantino is obviously a fan, and
he nearly put his own stamp on the character.

00:07:18.357 --> 00:07:22.819
At some point, Miramax bought the rights to
the "Modesty Blaise" series, apparently in

00:07:22.819 --> 00:07:26.669
the hopes that Tarantino would make a big
screen adaptation. Nothing ever came of it

00:07:26.669 --> 00:07:30.770
— so in order to keep from losing the rights,
the studio turned out a quickie direct-to-video

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:34.880
movie called "My Name Is Modesty." Variety
reported that the whole thing was thrown together

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:40.100
in 18 days, with a director who'd only been
on the job for a week before filming started.

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:44.151
Tarantino still hasn't shown any indication
of making his own Modesty movie, but he did

00:07:44.151 --> 00:07:46.900
get a credit on "My Name Is Modesty" as producer.

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:52.060
If there's another monologue in "Pulp Fiction"
as memorable as Ezekiel 25:17, it's the one

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:56.509
Christopher Walken recites during his single
scene as Captain Koons. He arrives in a flashback

00:07:56.509 --> 00:08:01.843
to Butch Coolidge's childhood to deliver the
boy's heirloom watch, along with its rather crappy history.

00:08:01.843 --> 00:08:06.680
"He hid it, in one place he knew he could
hide something, his ass. Five long years he

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:09.110
wore this watch up his ass."

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:12.810
One of the watch's previous owners, Dane Coolidge,
died in World War II at the battle of Wake

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:17.210
Island — but not before entrusting the watch
to an Air Force gunner named Winocki. The

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:21.220
man's name seems like an odd detail to include,
so it's no surprise that Tarantino didn't

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:26.099
throw it in on a whim. It's a tribute to Howard
Hawks, the multitalented Old Hollywood master

00:08:26.099 --> 00:08:30.449
behind movies ranging from romantic comedies
like "His Girl Friday" and "Bringing Up Baby"

00:08:30.449 --> 00:08:33.969
to film noir classics like "The Big Sleep."
The younger director paid his debt to his

00:08:33.969 --> 00:08:39.071
inspiration by placing the events of former
pilot Hawks' 1943 war flick "Air Force" in

00:08:39.071 --> 00:08:43.390
the "Pulp Fiction" world, inserting John Garfield's
character into Captain Koons' story.

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:48.060
"Pulp Fiction's" non-linear storytelling was
revolutionary in 1994, thanks in no small

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:52.480
part to editor Sally Menke's Oscar-nominated
work. Moments from early on in the film that

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:56.601
seem insignificant gain new meaning once we
see what happens before them. Quentin Tarantino

00:08:56.601 --> 00:09:00.580
didn't invent this style of storytelling — you
can see it in classic novels like James Joyce's

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:04.529
"Ulysses" and Kurt Vonnegut's "Slaughterhouse-Five"
— but he turned it from an intellectual

00:09:04.529 --> 00:09:09.780
exercise to a crowd-pleasing gimmick. In the
process, he inspired hundreds of imitators,

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:13.410
from Christopher Nolan's backwards breakout
hit "Memento" to the intersecting plotlines

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:15.242
of Paul Thomas Anderson's "Magnolia."

00:09:15.242 --> 00:09:19.000
But not everyone was such a big fan. In a
documentary extra included in one of Pulp

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:22.002
Fiction's DVD releases, Samuel L. Jackson
recalled —

00:09:22.002 --> 00:09:25.426
"I remember my mom saying, 'Why didn't they
put the movie together right? [...] It was

00:09:25.426 --> 00:09:30.020
jumping all over the place, back and forth,
you're dead, you're alive, you're back, you're

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:33.720
here!' I said, 'Ma, that's just how the movie
works.'"

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:37.520
Vincent Vega's name may sound familiar to
fans of Tarantino's first film, "Reservoir

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:41.850
Dogs." The only named member of the color-coded
crew is Michael Madsen's psychotic Mr. Blonde,

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:46.240
who's also known as Vic Vega. That's more
than just a coincidence — Tarantino envisioned

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:49.870
the two characters as brothers and wrote a
whole script exploring their relationship.

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:53.430
Madsen recently revealed some of the details
to The Hollywood Reporter: Vince and Vic would

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:57.230
have met up after being released from different
prisons and started a club in Amsterdam, neatly

00:09:57.230 --> 00:10:00.379
tying into Vince's legendary discussion of
the city in "Pulp Fiction."

00:10:00.379 --> 00:10:05.040
Unfortunately, other projects kept getting
in the way, and by now both Madsen and Travolta

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:10.270
are far too old to convincingly play their
own younger selves. The idea still resurfaced,

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:14.440
moving the timeframe from before the characters'
deaths to after and instead focusing on two

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:18.560
other Vega brothers who meet at their siblings'
funeral. For now, though, it looks like either

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:21.538
version will only ever exist in fans' imaginations.

